<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Lato</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.) · capital Lato</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Dorian hill city of eastern Crete with one of the best-preserved Greek town centres on the island, home of Alexander's admiral Nearchus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lato stood in a saddle between two hills above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mirabello">Gulf of Mirabello</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorian Greeks</a>, it was a city by the 7th century BCE, named after the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leto">Leto</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lato's remains include an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a>, a prytaneion, a temple and stepped theatral area, showing the civic life of a Cretan polis with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosmoi">kosmoi</a> magistrates. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nearchus">Nearchus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great's</a> admiral, came from Lato. Inscriptions record its wars and treaties with neighbours such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olous">Olous</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hierapytna">Hierapytna</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Lato's harbour, Lato pros Kamara, grew in importance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>. After the Roman conquest of 67 BCE the hill town declined and the coastal settlement became the main centre, forerunner of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agios-nikolaos">Agios Nikolaos</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lato.jpg" alt="Lato" width="320" height="229"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lato_-_Agora.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lato</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Eileithyia</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cretan Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lato pros Kamara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-384&amp;focus=lato" aria-label="Lato on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5330 906 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lato lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5650 1162L5632 1166"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5632 1166h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5650 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5650 1162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-384&amp;focus=lato">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

